---
/**
 * COVER — slice R1.
 *
 * Composition is the approved wireframe (Variant A), CONTEXT.md
 * Section Composition table, row "Cover":
 *
 *   credit line / name / tagline / roles / button   columns 1-7
 *   portrait, column-tall, full-height tall crop    columns 8-12
 *   vertical padding: flush at the top, 64px at the bottom
 *
 * The Cover is a cover, not a section entrance: no kicker rule and no
 * section numeral.
 *
 * IT IS INSIDE THE TEN-<h2> HEADING STRUCTURE, and the note that used
 * to sit here saying otherwise was wrong. CONTEXT.md locks "ten
 * sections, ten <h2>s, in visual order under one <h1>", and its
 * Section Composition table's ten rows OPEN with the Cover — so the
 * Cover is one of the ten, and the <h1> it also carries is the page's,
 * not a substitute for its section heading. Shipped with neither an
 * `id` nor an `<h2>`, which meant a screen-reader user walking the
 * page by heading met the page title and then jumped straight into
 * About, and the folio's first entry tracked an element that did not
 * exist.
 *
 * THE <h2> IS "Portrait", VERBATIM FROM src/data/sections.ts, and the
 * `id` is `sec-portrait` from the same entry — both read off the list
 * rather than guessed, because the rule is that a section's <h2> text
 * and its folio label are the same string. The Cover has no visible
 * title to carry it, so it is visually hidden with `.vh`, which uses
 * `clip-path` and stays in the accessibility tree. NEVER
 * `display: none` or `visibility: hidden`, which remove it from the
 * tree and defeat the whole point. It is authored AFTER the <h1> — see
 * the note at the element.
 *
 * `aria-labelledby` REPLACES THE HAND-WRITTEN `aria-label`. The
 * section was named "Dr. Syreeta McClain" by an attribute while its
 * heading said "Portrait" — two accessible names for one region, with
 * the attribute silently winning. Pointing the label at the heading
 * makes the region name, the heading and the folio label one string
 * instead of two, and keeps the landmark the aria-label was providing.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md CONFLICT, REPORTED NOT RESOLVED: this file's Composition
 * table row says the Cover takes no folio, while sections.ts carries
 * `01 / Portrait` for it and the folio therefore displays over it.
 * The entry is real and is left exactly as it is — see the R7 report.
 *
 * AUTHORED IN THREE BLOCKS ON PURPOSE. head / portrait / foot is what
 * makes the mobile stack fall out of source order — head, portrait,
 * roles+button — with no `order` property and no re-sequencing. A
 * two-block Cover (all text, then portrait) would drop the portrait
 * below the button on a phone, which is not a cover.
 *
 * NO grid-column or grid-row is set in this file. Every placement is a
 * collapse-aware primitive from global.css: .win-cover-head,
 * .win-cover-foot, .win-cover-portrait. Their collapse behaviour above
 * 1280, between 1024 and 1280, and below 1024 is stated in full in that
 * file, above the implementation.
 *
 * PHOTOGRAPH. `src/assets/portrait-hero.jpg` is byte-identical
 * (SHA-256 905825ac…) to `brand_assets/KNOW shoot 1.jpg` — black
 * blouse, white pants, grey seamless, clean negative space both sides.
 * That resolves CONTEXT.md's open filename item for this frame: the
 * working name is real and already on disk under src/assets. Nothing
 * was renamed. 3925×5887, EXIF orientation 1 (verified with
 * sharp().metadata(), not sips).
 *
 * GOLD. One saturated gold type accent is spent here: the word
 * "Legacy" in the tagline. CONTEXT.md's pinned budget names it
 * explicitly as one of the three ("the 'Legacy' tagline"), so this is a
 * budgeted use, not an addition. Zero hairline-gold rule contexts on
 * this slice.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portraitHero from '../assets/portrait-hero.jpg';

/* PASS F — THE LOCKUP REPLACES THE NAME.
 *
 * `?raw`, not <img>. CONTEXT.md's Logo entry: `currentColor` does not
 * resolve through an <img>, and the mark's colour is chosen at the
 * placement and inherited. THE FILE ON DISK IS NEVER EDITED — the two
 * replacements below are exactly the two CONTEXT.md sanctions, the
 * <metadata> block and the c2pa namespace declaration, and nothing
 * else. The root tag keeps its own viewBox, `fill="currentColor"` and
 * `role="img"` byte-for-byte; the class, the sizing and the
 * `aria-hidden` all live on a WRAPPER, so no attribute of the client's
 * artwork is rewritten at inline time.
 *
 * The C2PA manifest is inert and is stripped here, so it never reaches
 * the browser. 29 paths, one fill value, no <defs>, no <style>.
 */
import lockupRaw from '../../brand_assets/mcclain-lockup.svg?raw';
const lockupSvg = lockupRaw
  .replace(/<metadata>[\s\S]*?<\/metadata>/, '')
  .replace(/\s+xmlns:c2pa="[^"]*"/, '');
---

<section class="sheet cover" id="sec-portrait" aria-labelledby="cover-heading">
  <div class="win-cover-head">
    <!--
      PASS F. THE MARK IS A SUBSTITUTION, NOT AN ADDITION: it replaces
      the name's INK, not the heading. PASS E.1's candidate C set the
      lockup beside the <h1> and the Cover read as her name printed
      twice; the lockup IS her name, so it takes the name's place.

      OPTION CHOSEN: the <h1> KEEPS ITS TEXT, visually hidden in the
      bands where the mark renders, and the mark is aria-hidden
      decoration beside it. The alternative — the mark as the <h1>'s
      content, carrying the accessible name through an SVG <title> —
      was rejected for two reasons. First, it makes the page's only
      level-1 heading depend on SVG title exposure, which varies by
      AT and by browser, where a text node does not. Second, the mark
      is BAND-GATED (see the sizing note), so under that option the
      <h1>'s accessible name would come from the SVG above 1024 and
      from text below it — the page's most important string would be
      sourced differently at different viewport widths. Here the
      accessible name is the same text node at every width: "Dr.
      Syreeta McClain".

      The heading outline does not move. Same single <h1>, same text,
      same position in source, same <h2> after it: h1 -> h2 "Portrait"
      -> h2 "About".

      NO WHITESPACE between the two spans. A whitespace text node here
      is an anonymous inline box between two block-level children and
      would open a line box of 0.9em under the mark.
    -->
    <h1 class="cover-name"><span class="cover-name-text">Dr. Syreeta McClain</span><span class="cover-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={lockupSvg}></span></h1>

    <!--
      AUTHORED AFTER THE <h1>, NOT BEFORE IT. CONTEXT.md's structure is
      ten <h2>s "under one <h1>", and the page's only <h1> lives inside
      this section — so a section heading authored ahead of it would
      make the first heading a reader jumping by heading meets be a
      level 2, with the level 1 behind it. One line later, the walk is
      h1 "Dr. Syreeta McClain" -> h2 "Portrait" -> h2 "About", which is
      the outline this file's own comment claims.

      `.vh` is `position: absolute`, so it is out of flow: it is not a
      grid item, it does not move .sheet's auto-placement cursor, and
      it contributes nothing to .win-cover-head's flex column. Verified
      rather than asserted — .cover, .win-cover-head, .cover-name,
      .win-cover-portrait, .win-cover-foot, #sec-about and the document
      height all measure byte-identical to the pre-change build at
      1920, 1440, 1280, 1024, 1023, 900, 750 and 390.
    -->
    <h2 id="cover-heading" class="vh">Portrait</h2>
  </div>

  <!--
    column-tall. Full-height tall crop: the plate stretches to the
    section's own height, so the crop is TALLER than the file's native
    2:3 and never wider. No border, no shadow, no rounded corners.
    Eager + sync decode — a photograph that resolves late is absent
    from full-page captures and from any PDF the client generates.

    `<Image>` emits its own <img>, which never receives Astro's
    data-astro-cid-*, so the rule that sizes it is :global().
  -->
  <!-- No <figcaption>. CONTEXT.md's Photography section makes the Cover an
       explicit caption exception: the name is the caption, and the Cover
       takes no folio, kicker or section numeral either. The plate takes the
       height the caption used to occupy, so it now ends on exactly the same
       bottom trim as the button rather than 56px above it. -->
  <figure class="win-cover-portrait cover-portrait">
    <div class="plate-img cover-plate">
      <Image
        src={portraitHero}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain in a black blouse and white trousers against a grey seamless backdrop."
        widths={[560, 840, 1120]}
        sizes="(max-width: 1023px) 92vw, 38vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
    </div>
  </figure>

  <div class="win-cover-foot">
    <!--
      THE RULE, now the top edge of the foot cluster rather than the
      closing line of the name block. Smoked Slate hairline spanning the
      text window, columns 1-7. Zero gold.

      R1.2 put it low with nothing beneath it; R1.3 put it high, where it
      terminated the type and then the page stopped for half a screen.
      Both failed the same way — a rule needs something to belong to. As
      the lid of rule / roles / button it belongs to the cluster, and the
      open ground collects in exactly one place: between the tagline and
      the rule, bounded above by type and below by a line.

      Same element in the stacked layout, where it sits under the
      photograph and above the roles. One rule, both layouts.
    -->
    <hr class="cover-rule" />

    <!--
      NBSP binds each separator to the word BEFORE it. Without it,
      `text-wrap: balance` broke at the space in front of a middot and
      line 2 opened with a leading "· MOMAGER" — worse than the orphan
      it was fixing. With the bind, the only break opportunities are
      after a middot, so a wrapped line ends on the separator, which is
      the ordinary printed form of a run-in list. Copy is unchanged:
      same four words, same three separators, same order.
    -->
    <p class="role cover-roles">Educator&nbsp;· Consultant&nbsp;· Momager&nbsp;· Founder</p>

    <p class="cover-cta">
      <a class="btn-solid" href="#sec-contact">Work With Dr. McClain</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Flush at the top, 64px at the bottom — CONTEXT.md's per-section
     padding table gives the Cover 64. Sections MUST NOT share a single
     padding token, so this value is set here and nowhere else.

     The head row takes the slack (1fr) so the head sits flush to the
     top trim and the foot sits on the bottom trim. The portrait spans
     both rows, which is what makes the button and the caption land on
     the same bottom trim line. */
  .cover{
    /* One token for the bottom trim, because the portrait's bleed has to
       escape it by EXACTLY this amount. Two literal 64s would drift the
       first time one of them was edited. */
    --cover-trim: 64px;

    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
    row-gap: var(--s5);
    /* 80svh, not 100. A full-viewport floor plus a button pinned to the
       bottom trim plus ~324px of head content GUARANTEED a ~400px gap at
       any type size or arrangement — three passes redistributed the same
       space because the constraint, not the layout, was producing it.
       80 lands the remaining gap near 200px, which is a breath rather
       than a field, and it puts the next section's top edge just below
       the fold so the page has a reason to scroll. 78 was tested and
       reads slightly cramped under the tagline; 82 starts to re-open the
       hole. The plate still bleeds off the bottom trim — the section is
       simply shorter.
       R21 SLICE 1 SUPERSEDES THIS: 100svh, Jackson's ruling (option A).
       At 80svh the portrait ended 20svh above the fold and About's
       svh-derived top padding filled the gap with empty ground, so the
       photograph read as stopping short of the page. At 100svh the
       plate's bottom bleed lands on the fold. The hole R1.4 warned
       about is answered by the head's distribution below, not by
       shortening the section. */
    min-height: 100svh;
    padding-block: 0 var(--cover-trim);
  }

  /* ---- Head ------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* THE OVERLAP LIVES HERE. The head is raised above the portrait so the
     name's overrun paints ON the photograph rather than under it. The
     portrait is later in source order and would otherwise win.

     position: relative with NO offsets, purely to make z-index apply.
     CONTEXT.md's ban is on `position: absolute` with hard pixel offsets,
     which cannot survive a resize; this has no offsets to break.

     The z-index has to sit on the GRID ITEM, not on the <h1>. A z-index
     on the name alone would not lift it past a later sibling of its own
     ancestor — it would still paint inside an unraised .win-cover-head.
     This is a scoped rule on the element, not an edit to the primitive
     in global.css, which is untouched.

     Centred rather than top-aligned: see the void note below. */
  .win-cover-head{
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    align-self: stretch;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Top-aligned, NOT centred. R1.1 centred this to split one 270px hole
       into two 220px holes, which did not change what it read as. The head
       goes back to the top for a second reason as well, and it is the one
       that matters: it puts the name beside her HEAD rather than beside her
       sleeve, and that is where the clean backdrop is. See .cover-name. */
    justify-content: flex-start;
  }

  /* THE TOP INSET — PASS G.
     ========================
     The credit line used to be the head's first child and carried
     `padding-block-start: var(--s5)`, described here as keeping it "off
     the trim the way a cover line is inset from the head margin in
     print". PASS G deletes the credit line. The inset is NOT deleted
     with it: the reason it existed is a property of the head, not of
     that paragraph, and the mark is simply what is first now.

     SO NO NEW VALUE IS INTRODUCED. The same 64px moves from the
     departing element onto the container, expressed as the Cover's own
     `--cover-trim` rather than as a bare `--s5`, because the two are the
     same 64 and stating it as the trim ties the head's top inset to the
     section's bottom trim — they can never disagree, the same argument
     `.cover-portrait`'s bleed already makes.

     In bands 1 and 2 this also equals `--gutter`, so the Cover's first
     ink is inset one trim from the top and one trim from the left: the
     mark's box top lands on 64 and its ink lands on x = 64. In bands 3
     and 4 the gutter is 40 and clamp(24px, 6.4vw, 40px) and the top
     inset stays 64, which is correct — the bottom trim does not narrow
     in those bands either.

     ONE RULE, ALL FOUR BANDS. The band-4 override that used to re-state
     the credit line's inset is gone with the credit line. */
  .win-cover-head{ padding-block-start: var(--cover-trim); }

  /* The largest object on the page, and unambiguously so. The tagline
     this comment used to measure against is gone (PASS G); the ratio
     that survives is against 16px body copy, 8.25x at 1920, well over
     CONTEXT.md's 3x lede-to-body floor.

     opsz 144 with wght 300 and SOFT 15 — sharp, high-contrast, near-
     Didot. All four axes stated; none is left to Fraunces' defaults of
     wght 900 / WONK 1 / SOFT 100. These still govern bands 3 and 4,
     where the type renders and the mark does not.

     MARGIN-TOP IS 0, PASS G. It was `var(--s4)`, the gap between the
     credit line and the name. With the credit line deleted there is
     nothing above to be separated from, and the head's own
     `padding-block-start` is now what holds the mark off the trim. A
     surviving s4 here would stack on top of that inset and read as an
     arbitrary 104px. */
  .cover-name{
    margin: 0;
    /* Optical hang, measured not guessed. Round 1 put this at -0.055em,
       which set the box left edge to 57.2px at 1440 against a 64px trim
       and a 62.6px credit line — the D's stem then sat LEFT of every
       other left edge in the column. The correct value is the D's own
       left sidebearing, which was MEASURED off the rendered face rather
       than estimated: canvas TextMetrics.actualBoundingBoxLeft for "D"
       in Fraunces wght 300 at 123.84px is -4.663px = 0.0377em. At
       -0.038em the D's ink lands on 64.0px; the credit line's K lands on
       63.8px and the roles and button sit at 64. Four elements, one
       optical left edge. */
    margin-left: -0.038em;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-hero);
    font-weight: 300;
    /* 9.79vw, up from 8.6vw. This is a size change to a face whose four
       variation axes and optical hang are unchanged and stay unchanged:
       opsz 144, wght 300, SOFT 15, WONK 0, -0.038em.

       R1.2 asked for a 150-250px overrun without resizing, on the model
       that the box width was the limit. It was, at R1.1 — widening the box
       took the name from two lines to one. It is not any more: the box is
       already wider than the string, so widening it further does nothing.
       "Dr. Syreeta McClain" is a FIXED 6.762 x font-size wide, it starts at
       the page trim, and the plate begins at a fixed column line, so the
       overrun is determined by exactly one variable:
           overrun = 6.762 x font-size - 779.3   (at 1440)
       58px at 123.84px. 150px needs 137.4px of type; 174px needs 141px.
       There is no other lever, so either the type grows or the overlap goes.
       CONTEXT.md's Composition table now specifies the overrun and its
       Overlap rule requires it to be unambiguous, so the type grows.

       9.79vw gives 141px and a 174px overrun at 1440 — 33% of the plate. */
    /* PASS F hoists this into a token. The mark's width is derived from
       it (see .cover-mark), so the type and the thing standing in for
       the type read ONE value and cannot drift apart. The expression
       itself is unchanged. */
    --name-size: clamp(58px, 9.79vw, 152px);
    font-size: var(--name-size);

    /* THE <h1>'s RENDERED INK WIDTH, as a multiple of its own font-size.
       MEASURED, not calculated: a pixel scan of the rendered face at
       dsf 2 gives 6.7467 at 1920 (fs 152, ink 1025.50px) and 6.7454 -
       6.7497 across the six single-line band-3 widths, so it is a
       constant of the string in this face to four figures. It is NOT
       the advance ratio, which measures 6.7653 - the trailing -0.03em
       letter-spacing pulls the advance box inside the final n's ink.

       A canvas TextMetrics measurement of this string is WRONG and was
       discarded: canvas cannot take font-variation-settings, so it
       renders Fraunces at its defaults (wght 900 / WONK 1 / SOFT 100)
       with optical sizing free, and its ink/fs drifts 6.74 -> 7.88 as
       the size falls. Same trap as CONTEXT.md's "enumerate every axis"
       gotcha, one layer down. */
    --name-ink-ratio: 6.747;
    --name-ink: calc(var(--name-size) * var(--name-ink-ratio));

    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
    /* THE OVERRUN. The box is widened 34% past its 7-column window so
       "Dr. Syreeta McClain" sets on ONE line instead of wrapping, and the
       line is long enough to break the seam on its own — no type-size
       change was needed and none was made. Measured in the rendered face:
       the full string is 837.4px at 123.84px/-0.03em, the head window
       ends at 819.3px and the plate begins at 843.3px, so the line lands
       58px inside the photograph at 1440 and 61px at 1280 — a consistent
       ~11-13% of the plate across the band.

       A percentage margin resolves against the grid area's inline size,
       so the overrun stays proportional at every width in the band and
       there is no pixel offset to break on resize.

       Overridden back to 0 below 1280; see the collapse note. */
    margin-right: -40%;
  }

  /* ================================================================
     THE MARK — PASS F
     ================================================================

     THE SIZING RULE INVERTS HERE, and this is the round's whole
     constraint. Everywhere else on this page a mark's HEIGHT is set
     and its width follows (CONTEXT.md, Logo entry). This mark is
     standing in for a line of type that occupies a known measure, so
     its WIDTH is set from that measure and the height follows.

     WIDTH = the <h1>'s rendered ink width. Not its box (widened 34%
     by margin-right: -40%), not a column count, not a chosen size.
     One token, --name-ink, derived from the type's own --name-size.

     HEIGHT IS NEVER SET. The lockup's ink is 3.5582:1 and distorting
     her mark is prohibited by her guidelines. The box carries the
     viewBox aspect of 3.5692 and the browser derives the height from
     the width alone; measured, the rendered ink comes back at 3.5582.

     THE BOX IS 0.4186% WIDER THAN THE INK, and the correction matters
     because of what it lands on. getBBox against the stored viewBox:
     15.672uu of slack on the left, 0.393uu on the right, of 3837.64 —
     CONTEXT.md's stored 0.419% / 0.111%, confirmed rather than
     re-derived, and "essentially all of it on the left" confirmed too.
     So the box is grossed up by 1/(1 - 0.004186) for the INK to
     measure --name-ink, and hung left by the artwork's own left slack
     for the ink to LAND ON THE PAGE TRIM.

     That hang is the exact analogue of the type's -0.038em, which is
     the D's left sidebearing, and it exists for the same reason: the
     Cover's strongest alignment is four elements on one optical left
     edge at x = 64 — credit line, name, roles, button. Without it the
     page's largest object would sit 4.3px inside the other three at
     1920, which is visible. It is a proportion of the mark's own
     width, not a swept pixel constant, so it holds at every width.

     Colour is INHERITED. `fill="currentColor"` on the artwork's root,
     `color` from .cover-name (Obsidian), never overridden here. */
  .cover-mark{
    /* Gated OFF by default and switched on per band below — see the
       band note. Stated here so the phone case is the default rather
       than an override. */
    display: none;
    --mark-box: calc(var(--name-ink) / 0.9958140);
    --mark-hang: calc(var(--mark-box) * 0.0040839);
    width: var(--mark-box);
    margin-left: calc(var(--mark-hang) * -1);
  }

  .cover-mark :global(svg){
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    /* NOT set. The viewBox derives it. Stated explicitly so a later
       pass does not read the absence as an oversight. */
    height: auto;
    /* Tailwind's preflight puts `max-width: 100%` on <img>, and this
       file has already been bitten by it once (see .cover-plate img).
       svg is not in that rule today; released anyway, because a box
       deliberately wider than its parent's content box is exactly what
       that rule silently eats. */
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* ---- The band gate ----------------------------------------------
     BAND 1 (>=1280) and BAND 2 (1024-1279): the mark renders, the
     heading text is visually hidden.
     BAND 3 (751-1023) and BAND 4 (<=750): THE TYPE STAYS. The mark
     does not render at all.

     THIS IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A PREFERENCE, and it is the finding the
     round was told to look for. The lockup's thinnest sustained stroke
     is 0.76% of its own height (PASS E.1, carried forward, not
     re-derived), so it needs 161-210px of height for 1.25 device px at
     dsf 1; the shipped legible height was 168px. The derived height is
     --name-ink / 3.5582 = 1.8962 x --name-size, and it falls THROUGH
     that floor inside band 3:

         1920  288.2px      1023  189.9px
         1440  267.3px       901  167.3px   <- under 168
         1280  237.6px       900  167.1px   <- under 168
         1279  237.4px       800  148.5px   <- under 161
         1024  190.1px       751  139.4px   <- under 161
                             750  139.2px   <- under 161

     Band 3 is 751-1023 and the floor is crossed at about 905px, inside
     it. Gating mid-band would mean a new breakpoint, which is a layout
     move and is not this round's. So the gate is the band boundary
     that already exists, 1024, and band 3 keeps the type.

     CONTEXT.md is explicit that the width MUST NOT be raised to rescue
     the height — that distorts the composition to fit the artwork, and
     the measure is R1's, not the mark's.

     BAND 4 HAS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON. At 390 the name wraps to
     two lines and a lockup cannot wrap: held to the 340px measure it
     would stand 95.6px tall, 44% under even the permissive floor, and
     released to its derived width it runs off both trims — which is
     exactly the wreck PASS E.1 captured. The type stays.

     Gating is the page's existing move, not a new one: the masthead,
     the folio and all page motion are already band-gated.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px){
    .cover-mark{ display: block; }

    /* THE NAME'S INK IS REPLACED, THE HEADING IS NOT. clip-path, the
       same mechanism as the .vh primitive and for the same reason:
       `display: none` and `visibility: hidden` both remove it from the
       accessibility tree, which is the one thing this round MUST NOT
       do. position: absolute takes it out of flow with no offsets, so
       it stays at its static position and contributes nothing to the
       flex column; the <h1>'s height then equals the mark's exactly.

       Not the .vh class itself, because .vh is unconditional and this
       is band-gated. Same treatment, scoped. */
    .cover-name-text{
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      padding: 0;
      margin: -1px;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
      white-space: nowrap;
      border: 0;
    }

    /* The type's own optical hang comes OFF with the type. -0.038em is
       the D's left sidebearing and there is no D rendering here; left
       on, it would drag the mark 5.8px past the page trim at 1920.
       The mark brings its own hang — see --mark-hang. */
    .cover-name{ margin-left: 0; }

    /* R21 SLICE 1 — THE NAME DESCENDS, AND THE PHOTOGRAPH SETS HOW FAR.
       At 100svh the column is taller than its content and the slack has
       to land somewhere. Three distributions were built and captured at
       1440x900 on the build: name centred in the head, name grouped
       low on the foot, and name at the upper third. ALL THREE PUT
       MCCLAIN'S LAST GLYPHS ON HER HAIR: the photograph keeps its scale
       at 100svh and only shows more of her below, and her hair widens
       as it falls, so every row lower is a row closer to it. Measured
       against the pixels actually behind the glyphs: 1px clearance and
       1.7-2.2:1 at 1024-1440. The seam's clean backdrop is high in the
       frame, beside her head — R1's reason for top-aligning the head.
       SO THE DESCENT IS BOUNDED BY CLEARANCE, not by balance. Swept in
       steps of 0.1 x --name-size (the unit that tracks the plate's
       scale, both being vw-driven), at viewport heights 768, 900 and
       1080 — height matters, because the crop's `--crop-y` lifts the
       photograph further on a shorter plate. 0.2 is the largest step
       that keeps the overrunning glyphs at least --s3 (24px) clear of
       the subject at every width from 1024 to 2560 and every one of
       those heights: 31px at worst, glyph contrast 6.43:1 minimum.
       0.3 closes to 23px at 1279x768; 0.4 to 10px.
       Written on the <h1> because --name-size is declared here.
       Bands: >=1280 and 1024-1279 descend; 751-1023 and <=750 are the
       stacked Cover with min-height auto and no seam, untouched. */
    .cover-name{ margin-top: calc(0.2 * var(--name-size)); }
  }

  /* THE TAGLINE AND ITS GOLD ARE DELETED — PASS G.
     `Where Leadership Meets Legacy` and the `.legacy` <em> inside it
     are gone from the markup, so `.cover-tagline` and
     `.cover-tagline .legacy` are gone with them.

     THIS SPENDS ONE OF THE THREE SATURATED GOLD TYPE ACCENTS DOWNWARD.
     CONTEXT.md's pinned budget names three by name — "the 'Legacy'
     tagline, the About drop cap, the pull quote" — and this was the
     first. The page goes 3 -> 2. That is the authorised outcome of the
     ruling, not a side effect, and the two survivors are untouched.

     THE COVER NOW CARRIES NO GOLD AT ALL. Nothing is added back
     anywhere to compensate: not the role line, not the button, not the
     foot rule. See the report.

     Tokens that were used here and SURVIVE because others use them:
       --fx-display  -> SectionBlog.astro, PortraitBand.astro, privacy.astro
       --gold        -> About.astro's drop cap, FeatureQuote.astro, and
                        every focus-visible outline on the page
       --slate       -> .cover-roles below, and page-wide
       --s3          -> .cover-rule's bottom margin and .cover-cta below
     Nothing became orphaned in CSS. The `.legacy` CLASS NAME survives
     independently in FeatureQuote.astro, which scopes its own rule;
     comments in About.astro and EducationalLeader.astro still point at
     "the Cover's .legacy" and are now stale. Out of scope, reported. */

  /* THE RULE. 1px, Smoked Slate, spanning the text window. `margin-top:
     auto` in the stretched flex column drops it to the foot of row 1; the
     s5 bottom margin then lifts it back off the foot cluster.

     Its position is chosen, not defaulted to the midpoint. At 1440 that
     leaves ~276px of space above it and ~128px below (its own 64 plus the
     64 row-gap) — roughly 2:1. The larger share sits with the name block
     above, so the space reads as belonging to the name rather than being
     divided evenly between two clusters, and the rule reads as the line the
     roles and button sit under.

     `border: 0` and an explicit height because Tailwind's preflight styles
     <hr> as a zero-height element with a top border. */
  .cover-rule{
    width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    /* Tight to the cluster it caps: s3 down to the roles, s3 again from
       the roles to the button. Three elements at 24px read as one group;
       at s5 they read as three stranded things, which is what the last
       two passes shipped. */
    margin: 0 0 var(--s3);
    border: 0;
    background: var(--slate);
    opacity: 0.38;
  }

  /* ---- Foot ------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* balance, so the phone break is 2 + 2 rather than 3 + 1. At 390px the
     line is ~370px against a 340px column and must wrap; without this it
     wraps after "MOMAGER ·" and leaves "FOUNDER" alone on a line. The
     type size is not reduced to force one line — 12px is already the
     floor of the .role clamp. */
  .cover-roles{ color: var(--slate); text-wrap: balance; }

  .cover-cta{ margin: var(--s3) 0 0; }

  /* ---- Portrait --------------------------------------------------- */

  /* A column of its own so the plate can take the slack height and the
     caption can sit under it on the bottom trim, level with the button. */
  /* ---- THE BLEED -------------------------------------------------
     The plate runs off the right edge of the viewport and off the bottom
     edge of the section. A contained rectangle of studio backdrop sitting
     inside a page margin reads as an inserted element; running it off the
     trim is what a printed cover does.

     LEFT AND TOP EDGES DO NOT MOVE. The left edge is the seam the name
     crosses and the whole overlap depends on it being hard and where it
     is; it is not softened, feathered or faded.

     Escaped with negative margins sized to the exact values being
     escaped — var(--gutter) is literally the section's padding-inline and
     var(--cover-trim) is literally its padding-block-end, so the two can
     never disagree. NOT `width: 100vw` and NOT `margin-left: calc(50% -
     50vw)`: both measure the viewport INCLUDING the scrollbar and put the
     element past the content edge, which is horizontal overflow. The
     grid item is stretch-sized in both axes, so a negative margin simply
     grows the border box past its grid area.

     COLLAPSE, stated before implementing:
       >= 1024  right and bottom bleed, as above. Left and top hold.
       < 1024   the portrait is mid-stack, so there is no bottom trim to
                escape and the bottom bleed comes off. It bleeds to BOTH
                side edges instead — full viewport width, no gutter either
                side. It is the only element on the page that does this.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .cover-portrait{
    margin: 0;
    margin-right: calc(var(--gutter) * -1);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--cover-trim) * -1);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 0;      /* or the flex child refuses to shrink */
  }

  /* flex-basis 0, not auto. With `auto` the plate's base size is the
     <img>'s intrinsic height — 532.7px of column width at 3925x5887 is
     799px — and that intrinsic contribution reached the grid as the
     spanning item's min-content height, forcing row 1 to 674.5px and
     the section to 919px against a 900px viewport. Measured, not
     guessed: docH was 919 at 1440 and exactly 900 at every width below
     it, because only at 1440 was the column wide enough for the
     intrinsic height to exceed the slack. */
  /* ---- THE CROP -------------------------------------------------
     Measured off the source, not eyeballed. The subject's bounding box
     is x 650 -> 3050 of 3925 (2400px wide), leaving 650px of dead studio
     ground on the left and 875px on the right, and she sits centred at
     47.1%. Untreated backdrop is a flat #B6B6B7 across the full width —
     the "lighter strip at the right edge" is not in the file, it was
     being produced by the treatment ramp; see global.css.

     Two jobs: cut the right-hand dead ground, and seat her right of
     centre so clean backdrop opens on the LEFT, which is where the name
     crosses in. The crop and the overlap are one move, not two — the air
     the name needs is the air the crop creates.

     MECHANISM, and this is the load-bearing part. The first attempt used
     `transform: scale()` on the <img> and it SILENTLY KILLED the
     mix-blend-mode treatment. Measured on the rendered pixels: the
     treated backdrop went from #a5a29d (R-B 8, luminance 162) to
     #b3b4b4 (R-B -1, luminance 180) — which is the raw source colour.
     The reasoning that the transform was safe "because it is on a
     sibling of ::before, not an ancestor" is wrong in practice: the
     transformed <img> is composited into its own layer and ::before can
     no longer blend against it. CONTEXT.md's gotcha is broader than the
     word "ancestor" suggests. NO TRANSFORM GOES ON A PHOTOGRAPH ON THIS
     PAGE.

     So the crop is done by OVERSIZING THE IMG BOX instead — a width
     greater than 100% with a matching negative left, both percentages,
     clipped by .plate-img's overflow: hidden. No transform, no hard
     pixel offset, nothing to break on resize, and the blend survives.

     It also behaves better. While the box stays wider than the file's
     native 0.667 aspect, object-fit spreads the full source width across
     the box, so the visible source window is
         start = (|left| / width) x 3925      width = 3925 / width
     — plate width cancels out entirely, and the crop is IDENTICAL at
     every viewport width in the band instead of drifting with it.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .cover-plate{
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-height: 0;
    /* ONE crop for the whole desktop range, 1024 and up. 130% / -6.4%
       puts the source window at [194, 3213]: 456px of clean ground to
       her left, 163px to her right, and the entire right-hand dead zone
       cut away. She fills 80% of the frame instead of 61%.

       The window is constant down to about 1122px, where the box aspect
       (1.30 x plate width / plate height) finally drops under the file's
       native 0.667 and object-fit begins cropping inside the box as
       well. Below that it tightens CONTINUOUSLY to [374, 3085] at 1024 —
       so there is no crop jump anywhere in the range, including at the
       1280 boundary, and she is never clipped. An earlier version of
       this pass ran separate values above and below 1280 and stepped
       visibly at that 1px boundary; a single pair does the same job
       without the seam.

       Clearance for the overlap is what sets --crop-l, and the binding
       case is 1280, not 1440: the overrun is a LARGER fraction of a
       narrower plate there (13.1% against 10.9%), so the type reaches
       further into the frame at the low end of the band. At -6.4% that
       left 60 source px of clearance — 9 rendered px — which is a gap
       you can see closing. -5.5% buys 88 source px (13.6 rendered) at
       1280 and 164 at 1440, at the cost of 28 source px of air on her
       right. Worth it: black type meeting a black sleeve is a collision,
       a slightly tighter right trim is a crop. */
    --crop-w: 130%;
    --crop-l: -5.5%;
    --crop-y: 18%;
  }

  /* The <img> is taken out of flow so it contributes NO intrinsic height
     to the flex column or, through it, to the grid row — that was R1's
     919px-against-900px overflow and the fix has to survive this pass.

     Oversized box, no transform. --crop-w > 100% with a matching
     negative --crop-l crops horizontally; .plate-img's overflow: hidden
     clips it. X stays at 50% and does nothing: while the box is wider
     than the file's native aspect, object-fit lays the full source width
     across it, so all horizontal placement is --crop-l / --crop-w and
     the visible source window is identical at every width in the band.

     :global() — <Image> writes its own <img>, which carries no
     data-astro-cid-*, so a scoped selector silently stops matching.
     This has bitten this project twice. */
  .cover-plate :global(img){
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--crop-l);
    width: var(--crop-w);
    /* Tailwind's preflight sets `img { max-width: 100% }`, which silently
       clamped the oversized box back to the plate width and produced no
       crop at all — the computed width read 532.672px against a declared
       121%. Released explicitly. */
    max-width: none;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% var(--crop-y);
  }

  /* ---- Collapse ---------------------------------------------------
     Stated before implementation, for the overlap and the crop both.

     >= 1280   Full composition. Name on one line, overrunning 58-61px
               onto the photograph. Crop zoomed 1.19 and seated right.

     1024-79   THE OVERLAP STAYS ON. R1.2 cut it at 1280 because the
               overrun landed on her sleeve by 1152. R1.3's bleed widened
               the plate by a full gutter, so each plate pixel now covers
               fewer source pixels and the same overrun reaches less far
               into the frame. Re-measured across the band at 1279, 1200,
               1152, 1100 and 1024 — clearances in the R1.4 report. The
               wrap to two lines now happens at the same boundary the grid
               collapses, so the Cover has one breakpoint instead of two.
     < 1024    One stacked column, head -> portrait -> foot from source
               order. No overlap — there is no seam left to break, the
               photograph is below the type rather than beside it. The
               crop re-zooms because the capped 4:5 plate is wider than
               native again, and she is centred rather than pushed right:
               with no type crossing it, air on the left has no job.
               min-height goes to auto; a 100svh floor on a stacked cover
               adds dead ground under the button.
     --------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* No 1279px block any more. The overlap used to come off here; it now
     runs the whole 12-column range and stops where the grid collapses.
     See the collapse note above for the measured clearances. */

  @media (max-width: 1023px){
    .cover{
      grid-template-rows: auto;
      row-gap: var(--s5);
      min-height: auto;
      padding-block: 0 var(--cover-trim);
    }

    /* Both side edges, no bottom bleed — the portrait is mid-stack here
       and the roles and button follow it. */
    .cover-portrait{
      margin-inline: calc(var(--gutter) * -1);
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    /* PASS G: the credit line's inset override is gone with the credit
       line, and the name's s3 top margin with it — the head's own
       padding-block-start holds the top inset in every band. Only the
       overrun cancel is still needed here. */
    .cover-name{ margin-right: 0; }

    /* Nothing to raise above: the photograph is no longer beside the
       type, so the stacking and the flex centring both come off and the
       head is an ordinary block in the stack again. */
    .win-cover-head{
      position: static;
      display: block;
      align-self: auto;
    }

    /* Bleeds to both side edges. The 460px width cap that R1 added is
       gone with it, so the height needs a new guard: at 1023px a full-bleed
       4:5 plate would be 1279px tall, which is the 1.3-viewports-of-
       photograph problem R1 fixed by capping the width.

         height: min(125vw, max(100vw, 118svh))
           125vw  the 4:5 portrait ratio, which governs on phones
                  (390 -> 488, 360 -> 450)
           118svh caps it near 1.2 viewport heights on wide stacked
                  viewports (1023x900 -> 1062 rather than 1279)
           100vw  the floor that makes the whole expression portrait-SAFE:
                  height can never fall below width, so no viewport shape
                  can crop this vertical frame landscape. At 1023x768,
                  where 118svh would be 906 against a 1023 width, the
                  floor takes over and holds it square.
       aspect-ratio comes off because an explicit height supersedes it. */
    .cover-plate{
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      height: min(125vw, max(100vw, 118svh));
      /* 4:5 is 0.80 against the file's 0.667, so the box is comfortably
         wider than native and one pair of values holds at every phone
         width. Window [364, 3337] — centred, not seated right: no type
         crosses this plate, so air on the left has no job to do. */
      --crop-w: 132%;
      --crop-l: -12.2%;
      --crop-y: 12%;
    }
  }

  /* R20 -- THE CAP. Above 1600 the section's padding is no longer
     --gutter, so a -64px escape would leave the plate short of the trim
     by the whole surplus -- 480px of Porcelain at 2560, measured.

     100vw, and it has to be. The trim grows without bound with the
     viewport while the measure is pinned at 1472, so no percentage of a
     capped grid area can track it; a grid item's margin percentage also
     resolves against its own area, which yields -172.33px here instead
     of 544px.

     It is safe because a negative margin moves only the RIGHT edge.
     The left edge -- the seam the Cover's overlap is measured against --
     is the grid line and does not move. Verified under both forms at
     1600, 1920 and 2560, R20. body carries overflow-x: hidden, so a
     classic scrollbar's half-width overshoot is clipped, not scrolled. */
  @media (min-width: 1600px){
    .cover-portrait{
      margin-right: calc(-1 * max(var(--gutter), (100vw - var(--measure-cap)) / 2));
    }
  }
</style>
